# ============================================================================
# P02 - MEMORY: NR_CPUS 64 -> 8             [EXPERIMENTAL / UNVERIFIED]
# ============================================================================
# WHAT IT CHANGES
#   CONFIG_NR_CPUS: 64 (implicit arm64 default) -> 8.
#
#   baseline evidence (kernel source, commit 9b1c9a53):
#     arch/arm64/Kconfig:764  config NR_CPUS
#     arch/arm64/Kconfig:765          int "Maximum number of CPUs (2-4096)"
#     arch/arm64/Kconfig:766          range 2 4096
#     arch/arm64/Kconfig:768          default "64"
#     arch/arm64/Kconfig.platforms has no NR_CPUS override (grep: no match)
#     Neither MT6765 defconfig sets CONFIG_NR_CPUS, so 64 is in force.
#   The SoC has exactly eight cores:
#     arch/arm/boot/dts/mt6765.dts  cpu0..cpu7  (cpu@000 .. cpu@103)
#
# WHY IT SHOULD HELP ON A 4 GB MT6765V
#   NR_CPUS sizes the *possible* CPU count, not the present count. Many
#   per-CPU structures are allocated for every possible CPU: runqueues and
#   sched_domain cpu-bitmasks in kernel/sched, the softirq pending bitmap,
#   per-CPU softirq stats, per-CPU slab/slab paged caches in mm/slab_common.c,
#   percpu_counter fast paths, and the various *_cpu cache_line aligned
#   arrays scattered through drivers/. Every one of those is sized by
#   NR_CPUS, not by 8.
#   Second effect, and probably the larger one at runtime: the scheduler
#   iterates the possible-CPU mask on hot paths (for_each_possible_cpu /
#   for_each_present_cpu over sched_domain masks in kernel/sched/core.c
#   during load balance and idle-state recomputation). Going 64 -> 8 shrinks
#   those masks by 8x.
#   On a device with 4 GB of RAM, static kernel-side over-allocation is not
#   abstract - it is memory the compositor, ART and app processes cannot have.
#   This is a structural accounting change, not a performance guess: the
#   saving is proportional to (64-8) x (bytes per per-CPU slot).
#
# WHAT WOULD VERIFY OR REFUTE IT
#   Build must first succeed AND boot - a too-small NR_CPUS is a hard failure
#   (alloc percpu fails or the kernel panics on cpu_possible_mask overflow),
#   so a successful boot is itself the first test.
#   Baseline vs patched:
#     cat /proc/iomem | head          (percpu/percpu pool size)
#     grep -i percpu /proc/slabinfo  (percpu slab caches)
#     cat /sys/kernel/debug/sched/schedstats | head -1   (per-cpu rows present)
#     dumpsys meminfo | grep -iE 'Kernel|Used RAM'
#   Expect fewer per-CPU rows in schedstats (8 instead of 64) and a smaller
#   percpu pool. If /proc/iomem shows no percpu pool reduction, the patch did
#   not do what the rationale claims and should be dropped.
#
# RISK / GATE
#   MUST re-verify the target device's own DTS has exactly 8 cpu nodes. The
#   POCO M5 / Redmi 11 Prime 4G DTS is NOT the file read here (this is OPPO's
#   unified MT6765 DTS). If a POCO DTS ever declares more than 8, this must be
#   raised accordingly or the build breaks. Also confirm no out-of-tree MTK
#   module assumes NR_CPUS>8.
# ============================================================================

CONFIG_NR_CPUS=8
